# DESCRIPTION

k1-syscon drives two of the SpacemiT K1's system controllers, the units that gate its devices' clocks and hold them in reset: the APBC, at `0xd4015000`, for the peripherals on the APB (the UARTs, I2C, PWM, GPIO, SPI), and the APMU, at `0xd4282800`, for the application subsystem's (the EMACs, SDHCIs, USB, PCIe, DMA, display).
devmgr(8) starts it for each, before the devices that link to it, by its record:

```text
driver k1-syscon
class clock
match dt spacemit,k1-syscon-apbc
match dt spacemit,k1-syscon-apmu
```

It reads its node's `compatible` at `/dev/dt/compatible` to know which unit it has, and uses the first of its registers.
A device's `clocks` and `resets` name the unit and an id, the ids of `dt-bindings/clock/spacemit,k1-syscon.h`: the K1's first Ethernet MAC has the APMU's clock 37 (`emac0_bus`) and reset 35.
devmgr gives the unit's tree to that device's driver at `/dev/provider/<service>`, and its `/dev/dt/links` says which ids are the device's (devmgr(8)).

A clock is a gate, a mask of bits in its unit's control register: `on` sets them all, `off` clears them.
A clock with no gate (a fixed factor, a mux or a divider alone, such as the APMU's `pmua_aclk`) is always on.
A reset is asserted by writing its assert bits under its mask and released by writing its release bits: most of the APBC's are a reset bit, set while asserted, and most of the APMU's a release bit, clear while asserted.
Each change reads the register and writes it back with only those bits changed, one command at a time.
The APMU also holds each Ethernet MAC's control register, at `0x3e4` for the first and `0x3ec` for the second, which the MAC's node names by its `spacemit,apmu` offset, and after it the MAC's delay lines: `emac` sets the MAC's interface, RGMII or RMII, its reference and transmit clocks taken from the PHY, and for RGMII each delay line on with a code in steps of 15.6 ps (k1-emac(3) gives its node's `tx-internal-delay-ps` and `rx-internal-delay-ps`).
The first MAC's bus clock and reset are bits of that register too, so they are written only here.
The APBC has 102 clocks and 51 resets, the APMU 62 clocks and 53 resets.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/k1-syscon-dt-system-controller@d4282800` for the APMU, and at `/dev/provider/<service>` in the drivers of the devices that link to it.

: `ctl`
  Takes a command a line: `clock <id> on|off`, `reset <id> assert|deassert`, or `emac <offset> rgmii|rmii <tx> <rx>`, a MAC's control register and its delay codes (0 to 255), numbers in decimal or `0x` hexadecimal.
  A line it can't parse, an id or a MAC's offset the unit doesn't have, or `off` for a clock with no gate fails the write as invalid, and the driver logs which.
: `status`
  Read only.
  The unit, `apmu: 62 clocks, 53 resets`; then a line for each clock and each reset as its register reads now, `clock 37 emac0_bus on` (`off`, or `ungated` for one with no gate) and `reset 35 emac0 deasserted` (or `asserted`); then a line for each MAC, `emac 0x3e4 rgmii tx 0 rx 0`; then `commands <n>, failed <n>`, and `last failure: <command>: <why>`, the why `no such id` or `no gate`.

